---
/**
 * /about/ — TRUST PAGE. Built 2026-09-01.
 *
 * APPROVAL: brand/keyword-plan.md:279 (trust tier) and :349, listed with
 * /process/ as "still to build" in Wave 1. Never rejected.
 *
 * UNBLOCKED 2026-09-01. This page was deferred on 2026-08-31 as "genuinely
 * thin" because the company could state almost nothing verified about
 * itself. Three owner-supplied facts changed that: in business since 2018,
 * licensed and insured, and a service area covering all of Volusia County
 * and Orange County including Greater Orlando. `serviceArea` in
 * src/config/site.ts moved from null to a verified two-county array in the
 * same change that produced this page.
 *
 * TARGETS NO KEYWORD. Trust tier, same as /contact/ and /process/.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT THIS PAGE MAY SAY, AND THE EXACT SOURCE OF EACH CLAIM. Nothing on
 * this page is asserted from any other basis:
 *   - "since 2018"            → business.founded (re-verified 2026-09-01)
 *   - "licensed and insured"  → business.licensedAndInsured, no number
 *   - service area            → serviceArea + serviceAreaProse (verified)
 *   - what we do              → verifiedServices, flag by flag
 *   - the Moisture Check      → moistureCheck (verified 2026-08-28)
 *   - free estimate / paid check separation → verified offer separation
 *
 * OMITTED — not verified, so ABSENT rather than softened. An About page
 * invites every single one of these, which is why they are enumerated:
 *   [FOUNDER NAME / BIO / PHOTO] · [EMPLOYEE NAMES] · [CREW SIZE] ·
 *   [YEARS OF EXPERIENCE as a number — founded year only, never computed] ·
 *   [CERTIFICATIONS / TRAINING / MANUFACTURER APPROVAL] · [LICENSE #] ·
 *   [INSURANCE CARRIER OR LIMITS] · [PROJECT COUNT / JOB HISTORY] ·
 *   [REVIEW COUNT / RATING / TESTIMONIALS] · [AWARDS / ASSOCIATIONS] ·
 *   [WARRANTY] · [RESPONSE TIME] · [CUSTOMER STORIES] · [PHOTOS —
 *   openWallPhotos null, SEVENTH page waiting] · [PHONE — null] ·
 *   [HOURS] · [ADDRESS] · [HEIGHT / ACCESS / LIFT / SCAFFOLD — UNVERIFIED,
 *   TWELFTH build running] · [CREW/SUBCONTRACTOR STRUCTURE] ·
 *   [MUNICIPALITY LIST — county-level coverage was verified, not a city
 *   roster; expanding it would invent detail]
 *
 * THE FOUNDING-YEAR RULE, stated because it is the easiest slip on this
 * page: the year renders, the arithmetic does not. No "over seven years",
 * no "nearly a decade". `yearsExperience` is null and stays null.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * CANNIBALIZATION CONTROL:
 *   /services/ OWNS the service roster with per-page descriptions. This
 *     page names WHAT KIND of work the company does in one grouped
 *     paragraph and links there. It does not reproduce the eleven-entry
 *     list, and it carries no per-service description.
 *   /process/ OWNS the sequence — what happens after you make contact,
 *     step by step, and the free/paid ordering. This page states the
 *     PRINCIPLE behind that order (why diagnosis precedes pricing) in
 *     brief and links. It does not re-run the five steps.
 *   /stucco-moisture-testing/ OWNS the Check's method and readings.
 *   /stucco-repair/#pricing OWNS costs. NO price renders here, so
 *     `pricingDisclaimer` is deliberately NOT imported.
 *   /stucco-repair/#licensing OWNS the "licensed and insured is table
 *     stakes" argument. Named once here, differently, and linked.
 *
 * THE SPLIT: other pages answer what the work is, what it costs, and what
 * order it happens in. This page answers WHO IS DOING IT and WHAT THEY
 * WILL AND WILL NOT DO — the only page whose subject is the company.
 *
 * PLUS standing brand constraints: thermal imaging is a screening aid, not
 * proof; NOT a home inspection (FL licenses inspectors separately); no
 * standalone waterproofing or sealing (verified FALSE); no concrete
 * spalling, including by adjacency (verified FALSE); no emoji; no
 * exclamation points; company "we" voice throughout per the approved
 * voice profile.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import MobileStickyBar from '../../components/MobileStickyBar.astro';
import ContactCta from '../../components/ContactCta.astro';
import {
  business,
  serviceArea,
  serviceAreaProse,
  moistureCheck,
  licenseNumber,
} from '../../config/site';

const title = `About | ${business.name}`;
const description = `Stucco repair, replacement, and installation across Volusia and Orange County, Florida. Licensed and insured, working here since ${business.founded}. We find out why the wall failed before we price the repair.`;

/**
 * AboutPage + Organization. Only verified fields are emitted: name,
 * foundingDate, areaServed. No telephone, no address, no aggregateRating,
 * no award, no employee, no numberOfEmployees — all unverified.
 */
const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: title,
  description,
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'HomeAndConstructionBusiness',
    name: business.name,
    description:
      'Stucco repair, water damage repair, replacement, resurfacing, and new installation across Volusia and Orange County, Florida.',
    foundingDate: String(business.founded),
    areaServed: serviceArea.map((a) => ({ '@type': 'AdministrativeArea', name: a })),
  },
};

/**
 * The three commitments. Each is a restatement of something already
 * verified and already practised elsewhere on the site — not a new promise.
 * Deliberately phrased as things we DO and DON'T do, not as guarantees:
 * `warrantyTerms` is null and no guarantee language may appear.
 */
const commitments = [
  {
    title: 'We tell you when it is cosmetic',
    body: 'Not every crack is a job. Where the honest answer is that a wall needs watching rather than work, that is the answer you get, and it costs us the work we would have had by saying otherwise.',
  },
  {
    title: 'We keep the diagnosis separate from the sale',
    body: `Repair estimates are free. The ${moistureCheck.name} is a separate paid service, and it is never credited toward repair work — because a diagnosis worth less to us when it finds nothing is a diagnosis you cannot trust.`,
  },
  {
    title: 'We give you the findings either way',
    body: 'Readings, photos, and what they mean, written down. They are yours whether or not you hire us for the repair, and they are specific enough to hand to somebody else.',
  },
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

  <SiteHeader current="/about/" />

  <main id="main">
    {/* HERO */}
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">About us</p>

        <h1>A stucco company that looks behind the wall first</h1>

        <p class="lede">
          {business.name} has been repairing and installing stucco in Florida
          since {business.founded}. We are licensed and insured, we work across
          Volusia and Orange County, and the thing that makes us worth calling
          is what we do before we quote: find out why the wall failed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* WHO WE ARE */}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <h2>Who we are</h2>

        <p>
          We are a stucco contractor working in Daytona Beach and across
          Central Florida, in business since {business.founded}. Stucco is what
          we do &mdash; repair, water damage, replacement, resurfacing, new
          installation and the finish work around them. It is not a sideline to
          general contracting or painting.
        </p>

        <p>
          We are licensed and insured. So is every other stucco contractor
          worth hiring, which is why we mention it here and do not build a
          headline out of it. It is the box that has to be ticked before a
          conversation starts, not a reason to pick anyone.{' '}
          {!licenseNumber && (
            <>
              What is worth judging us on is the part further down this page
              &mdash; what we do before we give you a number.
            </>
          )}
        </p>

        <p>
          Florida is hard on stucco in a specific way. The rain arrives sideways
          and in volume, the humidity never really leaves, and a wall that lets
          water in does not dry out again the way it might elsewhere. Most of
          the failures we are called to are not really stucco failures. They are
          water problems wearing a stucco symptom.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* WHAT WE DO */}
    <section class="section alt">
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <h2>What we do</h2>

        <p>
          Our work falls into three broad groups. Repair is most of it &mdash;
          cracking, patching, water-damaged stucco, and the areas that fail
          first, like the joints around windows and doors, chimneys, soffits
          and decorative bands. Then larger-scale work: replacement and
          resurfacing, where patching a wall is no longer the sensible answer.
          And new stucco, on new construction and additions.
        </p>

        <p>
          We repair both traditional hard-coat stucco and EIFS, the synthetic
          system, which behave differently enough that telling them apart
          matters before anyone starts. We also paint stucco. Each of these has
          its own page setting out what the work involves &mdash; the full list
          is on{' '}
          <a href="/services/">our services page</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Two things we do not do, stated plainly because both get asked. We do
          not sell waterproofing or sealing as a standalone job; sealing may
          form part of a repair, but it is not something to buy on its own to
          fix a wet wall. And we do not do concrete spalling repair &mdash; the
          rusting-rebar failures seen on balconies and stair landings are a
          different trade, and you want somebody who does that work rather than
          somebody adjacent to it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* HOW WE THINK ABOUT THE WORK */}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <h2>How we think about the work</h2>

        <p>
          Stucco is a surface, and a damaged surface is usually reporting on
          something that is not the surface. That belief is the whole of how we
          work: we would rather spend the first part of a job establishing what
          went wrong than spend the last part of it explaining why the repair
          did not hold.
        </p>

        <p>
          In practice that means we settle the cause before we put a number on
          the work. Often the wall makes it obvious and a free estimate is the
          end of it. Where it is not obvious &mdash; where what you can see
          might be the whole problem, or might be what trapped water is doing
          to the outside of a wall &mdash; we test before we price. The{' '}
          <a href="/stucco-moisture-testing/">{moistureCheck.name}</a> is how
          we do that, and it exists so that nobody has to buy a repair on a
          theory.
        </p>

        <p>
          We are careful about what counts as evidence. A thermal camera tells
          us where to test and nothing more, so what it shows never reaches you
          as a finding on its own &mdash; what we hand over was measured. And
          what we do has a specific name and a specific limit: it is a stucco
          moisture assessment by a stucco contractor. Home inspection is a
          separately licensed profession in Florida and a different job, and we
          do not present ours as one.
        </p>

        <p class="aside">
          The order this happens in, from first message to finished work, is
          set out on <a href="/process/">how we work</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* WHAT YOU CAN HOLD US TO */}
    <section class="section alt">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>What you can hold us to</h2>

        <div class="commitments">
          {commitments.map((c) => (
            <div class="commitment">
              <h3>{c.title}</h3>
              <p>{c.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* WHERE WE WORK */}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <h2>Where we work</h2>

        <p>{serviceAreaProse}</p>

        <ul class="areas">
          {serviceArea.map((a) => (
            <li>{a}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>

        <p>
          That covers the coast through Daytona Beach and the rest of Volusia
          County, and west into Orange County and the Greater Orlando area. The
          failure patterns are consistent enough across this stretch of Florida
          that a wall in one part of it usually fails for the same reasons as a
          wall in another.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you are just outside it, ask anyway rather than guessing from this
          page &mdash; the counties above are what we have committed to in
          writing, not the limit of what is worth a conversation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* WHY CALL US */}
    <section class="section alt">
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <h2>Why call us</h2>

        <p>
          Because the cheapest version of this job is the one that gets done
          once. A repair aimed at the wrong cause is not a saving, however
          keenly it was priced &mdash; it is the same wall again in two years,
          plus what you already spent on it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You do not have to commit to anything to start. Tell us what the
          wall is doing &mdash; or email photos of it &mdash; and you get a
          straight answer about what you are probably looking at, including the
          answer that it looks cosmetic and needs nothing. If it needs more
          than that, we will say what and why before any money is discussed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <ContactCta />
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />

  <MobileStickyBar />
</Layout>

<style>
  .hero { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 4.5rem) 1rem; }

  .eyebrow {
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h1 { max-width: 22ch; }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .section { padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.25rem); }
  .section.alt { background: var(--paper-warm); }
  .narrow { max-width: 44rem; }

  .commitments {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
  }

  .commitment {
    border-top: 2px solid var(--accent);
    padding-top: 1rem;
  }

  .commitment h3 { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-size: 1.0625rem; }
  .commitment p { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  .areas {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.15rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .aside {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
</style>
